    "baseRefName": "main",
    "body": "## Scoped partial result; broad question unresolved\nA one-sided binary process has shifted laws converging weakly to the stationary mixture of constant paths, but under every coupling with that limit its coordinate mismatch probability tends to 1/2. Thus Thorisson Problem 3.3's illustrative synchronous metric-convergence proposal fails even in probability.\n\nThe broader request for some other two-process coupling characterization remains unresolved. This is not a counterexample to the distinct setwise-convergence problem 9900005. One substantive approach was used.\n\n## Independent review\nSeparate adversarial review: PASS for the scoped synchronous counterexample, with unchanged proof SHA-256 `7123c345d3ecdf4fecb8941596687da54c44e169831eb23177ea485fa8386722`.\n- 885 author assertions reproduced exactly\n- 3,044 independent exact assertions pass\n- Arbitrary-coupling, full-path conditioning, metric and dependent finite-offset arguments independently audited\n- Review code, receipts and required frozen proof snapshot included\n\nThe review is independent AI review, not formal verification, external peer review or a novelty certificate.\n\n## Source boundary\nThe complete relevant original section was recovered as indexed primary PDF text, but direct retrieval of the complete original PDF failed. Published-preview and Asmussen-source limitations are retained. Historical priority is unestablished. The ordinary Skorohod/Dudley family-of-copies theorem is not misrepresented as a single-path coupling.\n",
